example.paragraph:
The columns of $I_{2} = \,\begin{bmatrix}
      1 & 0 \\
      0 & 1
      \end{bmatrix}$ are $\mathbf{e}_{1} = \,\begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      0
      \end{bmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{e}_{2} = \,\begin{bmatrix}
      0 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}$. Suppose $T$ is a linear transformation from $\text{ℝ}^{2}$ into $\text{ℝ}^{3}$ such that

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{matrix}
      {T(\mathbf{x})} & {= T(x_{1}\mathbf{e}_{1} + \cdots + x_{n}\mathbf{e}_{n}) = x_{1}T(\mathbf{e}_{1}) + \cdots + x_{n}T(\mathbf{e}_{n})} \\
       & {= \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rlr}
      {T(\mathbf{e}_{1})} & {\mspace{9mu}\cdots\mspace{9mu}} & {T(\mathbf{e}_{n})}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\begin{bmatrix}
      x_{1} \\
       \vdots \\
      x_{n}
      \end{bmatrix} = A\mathbf{x}}
      \end{matrix}$$

example.paragraph:
$\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ rotates into $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {\cos\varphi} \\
      {\sin\varphi}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      0 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ rotates into $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- \sin\varphi} \\
      {\cos\varphi}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. See Figure 1. By Theorem 10,

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{array}{l}
      {T(\mathbf{x}) = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      {3x_{1} + \mspace{9mu} x_{2}} \\
      {5x_{1} + 7x_{2}} \\
      {\mspace{9mu} x_{1} + 3x_{2}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      ? & ? \\
      ? & ? \\
      ? & ?
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      3 & 1 \\
      5 & 7 \\
      1 & 3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} \\
      {\mspace{189mu} A}
      \end{array}$$
